  foam:{title:'Form Exploration with Foam',sub:'Exploration / Form & material',parent:'exploration',images:[g(8),g(12),g(11)],captions:['Foam form studies','A family of forms','Exploration in the workshop'],body:'We were told to make phone-sized forms simply to practise creating shapes, lines and surfaces.',prompts:['The starting point','Process & discoveries','Reflections'],notes:[{title:'The starting point',body:'We were told to make phone-sized forms simply to practise creating shapes, lines and surfaces.'},{title:'Process & discoveries',body:'After six weeks, many late nights, and countless hours sanding, cutting and polishing, we arrived at 15 forms we found interesting.\n\nI learnt a great deal about my taste in shapes and forms: building beautiful bodies through surfaces, lines and volumes; playing with space, contrast, tension and patterns; and analysing form more critically. It also strengthened my ability to handle long, focused design sessions.'},{title:'Reflections',body:'It was a difficult module and project, requiring many hours of work and effort, but it was extremely worthwhile. It felt like a critical point of improvement as a designer: knowing myself, knowing design and knowing forms better. No regrets.'}]},
  flour:{title:'Exploring Process with Flour',sub:'Exploration / Material & process',parent:'exploration',images:['assets/flour-process.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_1995.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_1996.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_1997.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_1998.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_1999.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_2001.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_2003.jpg','assets/flour-gallery/IMG_2104.jpg'],captions:['Flour and steam process study','Process study 01','Process study 02','Process study 03','Process study 04','Process study 05','Process study 06','Process study 07','Process study 08'],videoId:'78ZCLYIE9gE',videoTitle:'Exploring Process with Flour',body:'What kind of fascinating object of form can you make with just flour and steam, using process as the tool to mould it rather than our hands?',prompts:['The question','Material experiments','What the process revealed'],notes:[{title:'The question',body:'What kind of fascinating object of form can you make with just flour and steam, using process as the tool to mould it rather than our hands?'},{title:'Material experiments',body:'The key learning point for us was to diverge, then converge. We used different additives, ingredients, flours, techniques and systems, starting with wide and diverse creations. Week by week peer evaluations helped us narrow and refine the work into the final project: diverge, converge, diamond.'},{title:'What the process revealed',body:'The process was extremely enlightening. It helped me experience using trial and error to discover a beautiful result, and understand first-hand the value of repeatedly introspecting, reflecting and obtaining feedback for my work.'}]},
  cardboard:{title:'Building Furniture from Cardboard',sub:'Exploration / Structure & making',parent:'exploration',images:[g(19),g(22),g(9),g(13),g(6),g(27),'assets/cardboard/008.jpg','assets/cardboard/009.jpg','assets/cardboard/010.jpg','assets/cardboard/011.jpg','assets/cardboard/012.jpg','assets/cardboard/013.jpg','assets/cardboard/014.jpg','assets/cardboard/015.jpg','assets/cardboard/016.jpg','assets/cardboard/017.jpg'],captions:['The finished display','Structure study','Form in use','Making & assembly','Prototyping details','Working at full scale','Cardboard study 008','Cardboard study 009','Cardboard study 010','Cardboard study 011','Cardboard study 012','Cardboard study 013','Cardboard study 014','Cardboard study 015','Cardboard study 016','Cardboard study 017'],body:'The goal was simple: build a beautiful, functional magazine holder using only cardboard and simple instructions.',prompts:['The brief','Structure & assembly','What I learned'],notes:[{title:'The brief',body:'The goal was simple: build a beautiful, functional magazine holder using only cardboard and simple instructions.'},{title:'Structure & assembly',body:'Building the device was challenging. I had to keep the design simple to build, rigid and aesthetic, while working to precise measurements. This meant learning to use the CNC machine, experimenting with different cardboards, and relying on trial and error—prototyping in cheap cardboard before moving to the premium final-build material.'},{title:'What I learned',body:'I learnt how to work with a medium: using its strengths to inform the design, and treating its weaknesses not only as constraints but also as opportunities to create something more special.'}]},
